{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor systematically screened the newborn’s current condition (RC1), clearly separated inpatient infusion orders from home medicines and instructed not to use them (RC2), insisted on head‑ultrasound follow‑up and did not allow it to be skipped (RC3), used workspace records and asked for clear discharge documentation (RC4), assigned concrete owners/timing for follow‑up and emergency steps (RC5), addressed family refusal with practical alternatives (RC6), and verified caregiver understanding through teach‑back before closure (RC7). All seven responsibility‑chain items are met without any minor or serious error. Three high‑order points (HO1, HO3, HO4) were triggered and actively completed with meaningful trajectory impact, each supported by independent doctor‑action evidence beyond the minimum chain. No important non‑chain defect exists. Closure is safe, executable, traceable, and validly closed after teach‑back. The trajectory satisfies Strong (≥1 HO completed) and meets all criteria for Perfect (≥2 HOs completed with independent evidence), yielding grade 5.",
